B.anL=A.a(s(["The word 'Christian' was first used in Antioch around 43 AD to describe followers of Jesus (Acts 11:26).","Pentecost in 33 AD is considered the birthday of the Church, when the Holy Spirit descended on 120 disciples gathered in Jerusalem.","The Apostle Paul wrote his letters (epistles) between 49\u201367 AD, making them the earliest written documents of the New Testament.","The Gospel of Mark, believed to be the first gospel written, was composed around 65\u201370 AD, likely based on Peter's eyewitness accounts.","Polycarp of Smyrna (69\u2013155 AD) was a disciple of the Apostle John and one of the earliest church fathers. He was martyred at age 86.","Ignatius of Antioch (35\u2013108 AD) wrote seven letters to early churches while being transported to Rome for execution, urging unity and obedience to bishops.","The Didache, one of the earliest Christian documents outside the Bible (written ~50\u2013120 AD), provided instructions on baptism, fasting, and the Eucharist.","Justin Martyr (100\u2013165 AD) was the first major Christian apologist, writing defenses of Christianity to Roman emperors and explaining Christian beliefs to outsiders.","Irenaeus of Lyon (130\u2013202 AD) wrote 'Against Heresies', systematically refuting Gnosticism and affirming the four Gospels as authoritative.","Tertullian (155\u2013220 AD) was the first major Christian writer to produce an extensive body of Latin literature and coined the term 'Trinity' (Trinitas).","The Roman Emperor Nero (54\u201368 AD) was the first emperor to officially persecute Christians, blaming them for the Great Fire of Rome in 64 AD.","Origen of Alexandria (185\u2013253 AD) wrote the first systematic Christian theology and translated the Bible into multiple languages in his Hexapla.","The Apostle Peter is traditionally considered the first Bishop of Rome, making him the first Pope in Catholic tradition.","The catacombs of Rome, used by early Christians for burial and secret worship during persecution, span over 300 miles of underground tunnels.","Clement of Alexandria (150\u2013215 AD) founded the famous Catechetical School of Alexandria, one of the earliest Christian educational institutions.","Emperor Constantine I issued the Edict of Milan in 313 AD, granting religious freedom throughout the Roman Empire and ending Christian persecution.","The First Council of Nicaea in 325 AD, convened by Emperor Constantine, established the Nicene Creed and affirmed the full divinity of Jesus Christ.","Athanasius of Alexandria (296\u2013373 AD) stood alone against the Arian controversy with the phrase 'Athanasius contra mundum' \u2014 'Athanasius against the world.'","The Council of Constantinople in 381 AD expanded the Nicene Creed to include the Holy Spirit as fully divine, completing the doctrine of the Trinity.","Ambrose of Milan (340\u2013397 AD) was so popular that the crowd demanded he be baptized and made bishop even though he was still a catechumen.","Augustine of Hippo (354\u2013430 AD) wrote 'Confessions' and 'City of God', two of the most influential books in all of Western Christianity.","Augustine's mother, Monica, prayed for his conversion for 17 years before he finally became a Christian at age 32.","Jerome (347\u2013420 AD) translated the entire Bible into Latin, producing the Vulgate \u2014 the official Bible of the Catholic Church for over 1,000 years.","John Chrysostom (347\u2013407 AD), meaning 'golden-mouthed' in Greek, was famous for his powerful preaching and was eventually exiled for rebuking Empress Eudoxia.","The Council of Chalcedon in 451 AD defined Jesus as having two natures \u2014 fully divine and fully human \u2014 in one person without confusion or separation.","Pope Gregory I (540\u2013604 AD), known as 'Gregory the Great', sent missionaries to England and standardized liturgical music, giving his name to Gregorian chant.","The Venerable Bede (672\u2013735 AD) wrote the 'Ecclesiastical History of the English People', the most important source for early English church history.","Boniface (672\u2013754 AD), the 'Apostle of Germany', famously cut down the sacred oak tree of Thor at Geismar to prove to Germanic pagans that their gods were powerless.","The Great Schism of 1054 AD split Christianity into Roman Catholicism and Eastern Orthodoxy, primarily over the authority of the Pope and the filioque controversy.","Anselm of Canterbury (1033\u20131109 AD) wrote the famous 'ontological argument' for God's existence and developed the satisfaction theory of atonement.","The First Crusade began in 1096 AD after Pope Urban II's call at the Council of Clermont, resulting in the capture of Jerusalem in 1099.","Francis of Assisi (1181\u20131226 AD) founded the Franciscan order after hearing Jesus' call to 'rebuild my church.' He is known for his love of poverty and creation.","Thomas Aquinas (1225\u20131274 AD) wrote the 'Summa Theologica', a massive work that attempted to reconcile Christian theology with Aristotelian philosophy.","Julian of Norwich (1342\u20131416 AD) was the first woman to write a book in English. Her 'Revelations of Divine Love' described 16 visions she received during a near-death illness.","John Wycliffe (1320\u20131384 AD) first translated the complete Bible into English in 1382 and was condemned as a heretic. His followers were called 'Lollards.'","Jan Hus (1369\u20131415 AD) was a Czech reformer inspired by Wycliffe. He was burned at the stake at the Council of Constance in 1415 for refusing to recant his views.","Martin Luther nailed his 95 Theses to the door of the Castle Church in Wittenberg on October 31, 1517, marking the start of the Protestant Reformation.","Luther's translation of the New Testament into German (1522) took just 11 weeks and is considered a landmark of German literature and language.","Ulrich Zwingli (1484\u20131531 AD) led the Swiss Reformation and disagreed with Luther on the Lord's Supper, believing it was symbolic rather than containing Christ's real presence.","John Calvin (1509\u20131564 AD) wrote the 'Institutes of the Christian Religion' at age 26, one of the most influential theological works of the Reformation.","The Anabaptists, meaning 're-baptizers', rejected infant baptism and insisted on believer's baptism. They were persecuted by both Catholics and Protestants.","William Tyndale (1494\u20131536 AD) translated the New Testament into English from the original Greek. He was strangled and burned at the stake for this work.","The Council of Trent (1545\u20131563 AD) was the Catholic Church's response to the Protestant Reformation, clarifying doctrine and beginning the Counter-Reformation.","John Knox (1514\u20131572 AD) founded the Presbyterian Church in Scotland after being inspired by Calvin in Geneva, famously praying: 'Give me Scotland or I die.'","The King James Bible (1611 AD), commissioned by King James I of England, took 54 scholars seven years to complete and remains one of the most widely read Bible translations.","Menno Simons (1496\u20131561 AD), a former Catholic priest, became the leader of the Anabaptist movement in the Netherlands. The Mennonites are named after him.","The Puritans who sailed on the Mayflower in 1620 were Separatists who believed the Church of England could not be reformed from within.","George Fox (1624\u20131691 AD) founded the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers), emphasizing the direct inner experience of God without rituals or clergy.","John Bunyan (1628\u20131688 AD) wrote 'The Pilgrim's Progress' while imprisoned for preaching without a license. It became the second best-selling book in history after the Bible.","The Great Awakening swept through the American colonies in the 1730s\u201340s, with Jonathan Edwards and George Whitefield preaching to massive crowds.","John Wesley (1703\u20131791 AD) founded Methodism, traveled over 250,000 miles on horseback, and preached over 40,000 sermons in his lifetime.","George Whitefield (1714\u20131770 AD) is estimated to have preached to over 10 million people during his lifetime, making him one of history's most prolific preachers.","William Wilberforce (1759\u20131833 AD), a committed Christian, led the 20-year campaign to abolish the slave trade in Britain, which succeeded in 1807.","Charles Wesley (1707\u20131788 AD), brother of John Wesley, wrote over 6,000 hymns including 'Hark! The Herald Angels Sing' and 'O For a Thousand Tongues to Sing.'","Jonathan Edwards' sermon 'Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God' (1741) sparked a revival so powerful that people grabbed onto pews and pillars in terror.","William Carey (1761\u20131834 AD) is known as the 'Father of Modern Missions.' He went to India in 1793 and translated the Bible into several Indian languages.","Charles Spurgeon (1834\u20131892 AD), called the 'Prince of Preachers', drew crowds of 10,000 people in Victorian London and published over 3,500 sermons.","Hudson Taylor (1832\u20131905 AD) founded the China Inland Mission and was the first missionary to adopt Chinese dress and customs to reach the people more effectively.","The Azusa Street Revival (1906\u20131915 AD) in Los Angeles launched the global Pentecostal movement, which today has over 600 million adherents worldwide.","C.S. Lewis (1898\u20131963 AD), a former atheist, became one of the most influential Christian apologists of the 20th century through works like 'Mere Christianity.'","Dietrich Bonhoeffer (1906\u20131945 AD) was a German pastor who actively resisted the Nazi regime. He was executed just weeks before World War II ended.","Billy Graham (1918\u20132018 AD) preached the gospel to more people in person than anyone in history \u2014 approximately 215 million people in over 185 countries.","Mother Teresa (1910\u20131997 AD) founded the Missionaries of Charity in Calcutta in 1950 to care for 'the poorest of the poor.' She was canonized in 2016.","The Dead Sea Scrolls, discovered between 1947\u20131956 in caves near Qumran, contain the oldest surviving manuscripts of the Hebrew Bible, dating to 150 BC\u201370 AD.","The Second Vatican Council (1962\u20131965 AD), convened by Pope John XXIII, modernized the Catholic Church's liturgy, allowed Mass in local languages, and promoted ecumenism.","Martin Luther King Jr. (1929\u20131968 AD) was a Baptist pastor who led the American civil rights movement, drawing heavily on his Christian faith and the Sermon on the Mount.","The Lausanne Congress of 1974, led by Billy Graham and John Stott, united evangelical Christians worldwide around a common commitment to world evangelism.","The Bible has been translated into over 3,500 languages and remains the best-selling book in history, with approximately 5 billion copies distributed worldwide.","Today, Christianity is the world's largest religion with approximately 2.4 billion adherents \u2014 about 31% of the global population.","Africa has experienced the fastest growth of Christianity in history. In 1900, there were about 9 million Christians in Africa. Today there are over 600 million.","The Apostles' Creed, one of the oldest Christian creeds, dates to the 2nd century and is still recited in churches around the world today.","The word 'Canon' in reference to the Bible comes from the Greek word for 'measuring rod.' The New Testament canon was largely settled by the 4th century.","Athanasius of Alexandria first listed the 27 books of the New Testament \u2014 the same ones we have today \u2014 in his Festal Letter of 367 AD.","The theological term 'heresy' comes from the Greek word for 'choice.' Early church heretics were those who chose to deviate from accepted doctrine.","Gnosticism, one of the earliest heresies, taught that the material world was evil, salvation came through secret knowledge, and Jesus only appeared to be human.","The practice of singing hymns in Christian worship dates back to the New Testament itself \u2014 Paul and Silas sang hymns in prison (Acts 16:25).","Monasticism began in Egypt with Anthony of the Desert (251\u2013356 AD), who retreated to the wilderness to seek God away from worldly distractions.","Benedict of Nursia (480\u2013547 AD) wrote the 'Rule of Benedict', a guide for monastic life that shaped Western monasticism for over 1,500 years.","The word 'liturgy' comes from the Greek 'leitourgia' meaning 'work of the people.' Early Christian worship was highly participatory and communal.","The practice of Sunday worship began in the earliest church because Jesus rose from the dead on the first day of the week (John 20:1).","Stephen was the first Christian martyr, stoned to death around 34 AD. A young man named Saul (later Paul) watched over the cloaks of those who killed him.","Perpetua and Felicity were martyred in Carthage in 203 AD. Perpetua kept a diary of her imprisonment that survives to this day.","Patrick of Ireland (387\u2013461 AD) was kidnapped as a youth and enslaved in Ireland. After escaping, he returned as a missionary and converted much of the island.","Columba (521\u2013597 AD) founded the famous monastery on the Isle of Iona, Scotland, which became a major center for Celtic Christianity and missionary activity.","The martyrdom of the 'Uganda Martyrs' in 1885\u20131887 \u2014 45 young Christian converts killed by King Mwanga \u2014 sparked a revival that transformed Uganda.","Jim Elliot and four other missionaries were killed by the Huaorani people of Ecuador in 1956. His wife Elisabeth later went back to minister to the same tribe.","Jim Elliot's famous journal entry reads: 'He is no fool who gives what he cannot keep to gain what he cannot lose.'","The Moravian Brethren, founded in 1727 under Count Zinzendorf, sustained an unbroken prayer chain for over 100 years and sent missionaries around the world."]),t.s)
